again I'd like to express my  appreciation for the invitation to be  here I am honored to have this  opportunity to share a word with you and  I pray that you would pray with me as we  reflect on the theme celebrating god's  grace and mercy amid tough times last  Sunday I had the awesome privilege of  attending a unique presentation by the  renowned Harlem visual arts and social  activist faith Ringgold she was  presenting at the New York metropolitan  museum of art and as she highlighted her  role in designing the 911 p story quilt  which was completed in collaboration  with a number of New York children who  had been traumatized like much of  America by the terrorist attacks of  September 11 2001 I hung on her every  word it was in response to this tribe  these tragic events that the  interrelations collaborative spear hated  head spill head spearheaded by a grace  yone gathered together children who were  ages 11 through 19 from diverse  backgrounds nationalities and religious  traditions and created a safe space for  them to share their experiences of 911  from their words and images they  authored also with the support of  Ringgold the insightful book which  inspired the quilt titled what will you  do for peace impact of 911 on New York  City you moved by the sincerity of the  children's work ringgold writes in the  introduction of the book when I was  shown the layout for a new book titled  what will you do for peace my heart was  filled with joy what a beautiful  collaboration  perfect response from New York's young  people this gracefully poetic account of  that frightening day and their young  lives is a gift of sensitivity and love  I was amazed at their generosity of  spirit I found the paintings and  expressive verse in the book deeply  inspiring as I scurried away from  Ringgold's presentation with my  colleagues and friends to view the 911  peace story quilt which was also on  display in the museum I was steadily  suddenly struck by the realization that  regardless of who we are it doesn't  matter how old we are what nationality  we are whether we're rich or poor  educated or illiterate at some point in  our lives we will all experience tough  times some may experience a personal  tragedy as they mourn the loss of a  loved one others will receive  heartbreaking news about a debilitating  illness and many in our country are  experiencing financial troubles brought  on by a waning economy and joblessness  and nearly a month ago families in our  own state of Connecticut experienced  utter devastation as they lost their  homes their businesses and everything  they owned due to the impact of  hurricane or tropical storm irene  unfortunately for the piece quilt  artists tough times entered their lives  at a very young age and turned it upside  down we were glued to the TV one child  wrote when we heard a plane crashed into  the Pentagon we started to cry I will  never forget my mom's face it will live  like thorns  in my heart when I turned on the TV  another wrote all I see was news on  every channel I was praying for the  people who died I thought about them and  fill another observed the day of sep  tember 11th was one of the worst days of  my life it made me feel sad for all of  the families who lost a dad mom and  uncle grandmother any family member I  just never actually suspected that  anything like this could happen yes some  of us will encounter bird was so great  that we may often wonder if we will ever  make it through in Psalm 145 David  offers us a ray of hope by shifting our  attention from our burdens to a litany  of God's Beauty Majesty and wondrous  works he reminds us that we serve a  great god a god who was able to do far  more than we can ever think or dream or  even imagine God is gracious and  merciful compassionate and loving and no  matter how hopeless a situation seems we  can trust in God's ability to bring us  through the most difficult times in our  lives in a similar fashion when Jonah  preached to the people of Nineveh it  seemed that there was no hope the city  would be destroyed because of their  wicked ways but when the people turn  from their sin God changed God's mind  and decided instead to save the city the  people of Nineveh experienced firsthand  not only God's grace and mercy but God's  power to turn a dire situation around  and then Jesus our Savior was on the  brink of  utter destruction he was mocked beaten  and crucified on the cross he was buried  in a borrowed tomb and after his death  it seemed that all was lost but early  Sunday morning he rose from the dead  conquering life's ultimate devastation  so when Christ we have hope for a better  day in Christ we have the power to  overcome life life's most difficult  situations and in Christ we have the  gift of eternal life like so many people  my journey has not always been easy but  i'm learning to celebrate god's grace  and mercy amid tough times but how you  may ask can we celebrate god's grace and  mercy amid tough times i believe that we  can begin by looking for glimpses of  god's grace and mercy wherever we go  when we are feeling low look for God's  grace and mercy in a golden sunset in  the laughter of children playing in our  community service or in an engaging  classroom dialogue we can also celebrate  God's grace and mercy by sharing acts of  kindness with others whatever we do does  it have to be big a simple touch a warm  embrace a heartfelt smile will do we can  further celebrate God's grace and mercy  by becoming instruments of God's love  compassion peace and justice and  ultimately we can celebrate God's grace  and mercy by reaching out to those in  need and becoming the answer to prayer  and someone's life in recent days the  911 artists  have become a symbol for me of  celebrating god's grace and mercy amid  tough times for even though they were  surrounded by tremendous violence  destruction and despair after the terror  attacks the young New Yorkers came  together from different cultures and  religions across New York City and  transformed their disastrous and  divisive experiences of 911 into a  healing uplifting and inspiring project  for peace thus their vision for peace  and hope inspires us to not only  proclaim God's goodness but to embody  God's love and God's peace compassion  and justice in our world as we gather  around the communion table this Sunday  let us celebrate God's grace and mercy  and a world that is enveloped by  tremendous sorrow and pain as we  celebrate communion let us remember  those in the midst of a severe famine in  Kenya and those families that are still  trying to piece together their worlds  from the devastating earthquakes in  haiti and japan let us remember people  all over the world battling the hiv/aids  pandemic and let us remember all who are  impacted by the wars in Afghanistan and  Iraq and most of all let us not forget  those among us in our own communities  who are suffering daily as we gather  this morning as a community of faith let  us also celebrate the hope that is  within us the hope of the psalmist who  proclaims to a people living in utter  destruction and despair that God is  gracious and merciful slow to anger  and abounding in steadfast love the hope  of the children who find glimpses of  God's grace and mercy as they strive for  peace and the hope in a risen Savior who  reminds us this communion sunday that  because he lives we can celebrate God's  grace and mercy amid tough times
 

